# Project Name

## Start Here

```bash
.claude/bin/tasks bootstrap          # loads mind map, skills, pending tasks
```

Then **ask the user** what they want to work on. Don't autonomously pick a task.

## Mind Map

Read `MIND_MAP.md` at project root before starting any work. It's the project's institutional memory — architecture, decisions, and reasoning. Consult it before each task, update it after completing one. `tasks bootstrap` prints it (an index of routing nodes + titles once it's large); pull a node's full content — across `MIND_MAP.md` and the `MIND_MAP_OVERFLOW.md` deep-detail tier — with `.claude/bin/tasks recall <N>`, or locate nodes by topic with `tasks recall <keyword>`.

## Choosing the ceremony level (decide this yourself — don't make the user pick)

Match the process to the RISK and size of the request. **You classify it; the user should not have to.** When you're on the line between two levels, always choose the heavier one — better safe than sorry.

- **No code touched** (a question, docs, a shell command, git, reading) → just do it, no task.
- **Trivial and clearly reversible code** (a typo, a rename, a small self-contained pure function) → `tasks new quick` (3 gates, no review; closes clean).
- **Small but touches real behavior/logic, OR touches a claim/config/data, OR you're not certain it's reversible** → `tasks new light` (risk classified first, review routed by risk).
- **Multi-step, a new subsystem, an uncertain approach, or anything irreversible / assertive / security-relevant** → a full task type (`feature`/`build`/`bugfix`/`research`/…).
- **The user explicitly wants to pair with no gate pressure** → `tasks freehand`. (Don't pick this on your own — it keeps no trace, so it's the least safe option; use it only when the user asks.)

**Only when you genuinely cannot tell the risk or the scope**, ask the user which level — but always lead with a recommendation, and let that recommendation default to the SAFER (heavier) option. Never pick a lighter level just to save time on something that might not be reversible.

## Task Lifecycle

These are **your** commands to run — the user never calls the tasks CLI.

Once you've chosen the level (above): run `tasks new <type>` then immediately `tasks work <N>` — never leave a task created but unactivated.

```
tasks new <type> <name> [intent]   # creates task.md — then immediately:
tasks work <N>                     # activate it — hooks start enforcing
```

Work through task.md gates: Design Phase (understand, structure, verify) → Work Plan (build/investigate gates) → Pre-review. Check each gate's checkbox as you complete it, appending your observations on the same line.

When done: `tasks work done` — runs the project's verify contract, records a receipt, sets status to done.

The task.md **is** the execution trace. Never skip checkboxes. Never backfill. Close each gate as you complete it; closing several ALREADY-DONE gates in one edit is allowed only when each line carries its own outcome note (hook-enforced, max 5 — a bare batch tick is blocked; where no hook runs, keep to one gate at a time).

## Correctness Contract

The close is **earned, not asserted** — these are enforced by the CLI, not conventions:

- **`## Risk`** — set at the Structure gate: `reversible` (the WORLD reverts, not the diff — only code/config that `git revert` fully undoes; persisted data, on-disk formats, secrets, history, and published claims never qualify even when the diff reverts cleanly), `irreversible` (deletes/migrates data, publishes — needs a named rollback plan), or `assertive` (changes a **claim about the world**: docs, a calibration, a "verified accurate"). Assertive/irreversible tasks **cannot close without an implementation review** — a small diff is not a reason to skip review; a docs-only diff can be the most review-worthy thing a task produces.
- **Verify contract** — declare in `.agent/config.json` what green means for this project, and closes run it:
  ```json
  {"verify": {"_always": ["<your test command>"], "assertive": ["<claim checks>"]}}
  ```
  A failing verify blocks the close. `--force` requires `--reason "why"` — recorded in the receipt.
- **`tasks audit`** — run before any review: mechanical sweeps (conflict markers, merge artifacts, stale markers, mind-map drift) so judges spend tokens on hard problems, not greppable ones. Reviews warn when the audit is missing or stale.
- **Judges are advisors, not oracles.** Triage every finding (accept/park/reject with rationale). A clean panel does NOT clear correspondence (does it match the world? check the real artifact), disclosure (secrets/provenance — a grep's job), or blast radius (the `## Risk` gate's job).
- **Test claims need executable proof.** A test you haven't watched fail is not evidence; assert against real output, not a mock you wrote.
- **Judges can execute what you declare safe.** Add `"judge_verify": ["<command>"]` to `.agent/config.json` — commands that never write inside the repo (redirect caches, unique temp dirs, parallel-safe) — and reviewers may run them to check a specific suspicion. Write your tests to qualify.
- **Keep task.md reviewable.** Judges read it through a bounded window (newest sections + Intent/Design survive trimming; receipts in judge.md say exactly what was delivered). When a task file grows past the budget, `tasks audit` nudges the sanctioned compaction: wrap old review-round narrative in `<!-- archive:start -->` … `<!-- archive:end -->` and run `tasks compact <N>` — it moves each block VERBATIM to `task-archive.md` and refuses to touch gates, Intent/Design/Parked, or pins. A section that must survive any trim gets `<!-- pin -->` on its own line right under the heading.

## CLI

```bash
.claude/bin/tasks work <number>              # activate task, hook starts tracking
.claude/bin/tasks work done                  # verify + receipt + deactivate (--force needs --reason)
.claude/bin/tasks new <type> <name>          # create task — does NOT activate
.claude/bin/tasks list [--pending]           # task overview
.claude/bin/tasks status                     # current gate position
.claude/bin/tasks audit [<N>]                # mechanical pre-review sweeps + receipt
.claude/bin/tasks blocked "<reason>"         # honest pause: awaiting the user's decision
.claude/bin/tasks parked [--all]             # surface parked items before they rot
.claude/bin/tasks compact <N>                # move <!-- archive:start/end --> narrative to task-archive.md
.claude/bin/tasks recall <id|words>          # fetch a mind-map node (main+overflow) or locate nodes by keyword
.claude/bin/tasks bootstrap                  # orientation: mind map (indexed if large) + skills + pending
```

## Don't

- Create task directories manually — always `.claude/bin/tasks new`
- Edit session state files directly (under `.agent/`, or `.agent/<user>/` in a multi-user repo) — use `.claude/bin/tasks work <N>` / `.claude/bin/tasks work done`
- Edit `## Status` in task.md directly — use `.claude/bin/tasks work done` (or `tasks blocked` for an honest pause)
- Skip task.md checkboxes — they're your observable progress
- Start coding without an active task — blocked by hook until `.claude/bin/tasks work <N>`
- Fake a checkbox to end a turn — if the next gate needs the user's decision, `tasks blocked "<reason>"` is the honest state
- Leave the mind map describing code that no longer exists — update the owning node in place when you change the architecture
